      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rome, Ohio earned its name from Rome, Italy, a nod to classical heritage that early settlers cherished.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rome%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Revolutionary War veteran Obadiah Stout was the first white settler in the area, establishing a homestead on the Ohio River in 1796.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rome_ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Rome’s layout reflects its river roots; the Ohio River’s wide channel once made it a bustling landing point before railroads shifted trade routes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rome_ohio",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "William Stout laid out Rome in 1835, choosing a spot that offered fertile land and a strategic position along the Ohio River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rome%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Elisha Stout, a native of Rome, went on to help found Omaha, Nebraska, and Denver, Colorado, linking the village to western expansion.",
